#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082983 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d887d9f-b9c4-54b3-a53e-691989bda962" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Odd Functions Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "66135849-b0f8-53a9-aab8-4b6aebde3cd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3994" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b6c7717b81ff0b7af12e3a247ecee69bb61bdd79fdea79ac5a7e769e4a3f1fd4.png)\n\nWhich of the above show the graph of an odd function?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graphs I, II, and IV do not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, they are **not** odd functions.\n\n- On the other hand, graph III does have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graph $180^\\circ$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that graph III shows an odd function.\n\n![](images/9e/9eb4d72e1fbcc0fdefbca6301127be3bb98c6a7a8bc1b4bffb66144fe9c15010.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082984 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bff4063-9e51-53fa-ab55-e68540e6f587" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Given the Table for an Even or Odd Function"}] :question/id #uuid "5b22df97-b731-5d77-a6ed-2a90efcd95a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3996" :question/problem "The table below defines the function $g(x).$ Given that $g(x)$ is an even function, find the value of $a+b.$\n\n| **$x$** | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $20$ | $11$ | $-4$ | $a-3$ | $11$ | $b+5$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $g(x)$ is an even function, we must have that $g(-x)=g(x).$ \n\nUsing the fact that $g(-1) = g(1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-1) & =g(1) \\\\ -4 & =a-3 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $g(-5) = g(5),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-5) & =g(5) \\\\ 20 & =b+5 \\\\ b & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b = -1 + 15 = 14.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082992 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb0d0f84-a628-5605-a75f-f4f5459e047c" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Even Functions Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "f8d678e6-c0db-534d-9ae2-0551ea8201bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4172" :question/problem "![](images/24/24c2b0f28f2ae9eae92527191f0e97fb87aa1eb74323cf2d4b7f2f5fc2ecdd71.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of an even function?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of an even function must be symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n- Graphs II, III, and IV are not symmetric about the $y$-axis. So, they are **not** even functions.\n\n- On the other hand, graph I is symmetric about the $y$-axis. That is to say, if we reflect graph I over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that graph I is an even function.\n\n![](images/b2/b2598584b088e924dc518aa39d4adb1966f5670c827389c90bbf089dc50ee5e8.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082993 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083185 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fa847d9-f0bf-57fb-92b2-27c8a7cdb3b1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Even and Odd Functions Using the Algebraic Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "373bdf54-aaa4-5e94-82a7-95c0a396623a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4183" :question/problem "Is the function $f(x)=|x| -1$ even, odd, both, or neither?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all $x,$ while\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x) = -f(x)$ for all $x.$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =|(-x)|-1 \\\\  & =|x|-1 \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x) = f(x),$ the function is even.\n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(|x|-1) \\\\  & =-|x|+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x) \\neq -f(x),$ the function is not odd.\n\nTherefore, the function is only even."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb0d0f84-a628-5605-a75f-f4f5459e047c" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Even Functions Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0676e816-698c-55c5-97c5-426cf8a46841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/164bdf0340e40fc342842d9476628d8751e473821b22fd3eda5f01d1dac195c0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518999} {:answer/id #uuid "1f179696-1b1a-5a79-9215-1690b67b0ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6fb301e3efc0981d79a834e8c42e341a7d379300248a7a818bfa39b116d4e862.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519043} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa4a4f9-72dc-528e-b63d-bd04e03e76bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7baa5fbbb5d65b585dd97566ea1da6f9470f848b606dbaeadba323f02fde4a96.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519081} {:answer/id #uuid "83d223bd-390e-5184-89db-83314d052609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/18/186c6b4f4c7674d4792e0ab00ee69eb2bac361f2fd43392725ede7f8203f067c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519198} {:answer/id #uuid "91db3be4-c008-53c8-a655-510128d6f235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/229e552353fbff6fb94b201c6924d3f3851bae102fcad9dab3de83a4a9bea05f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f179696-1b1a-5a79-9215-1690b67b0ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6fb301e3efc0981d79a834e8c42e341a7d379300248a7a818bfa39b116d4e862.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519043} :answer-field/id #uuid "234e0980-b8d4-52ce-bfbd-22840a3538a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc5a7bfe-60f8-5841-b9f2-93c7cc229a66" :question/math-academy-id "q-110598" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0189edb7d5582896a6b56623fa5b33c1dc9b65ff3d8ccaf634df452a72d9e0f.png)\n\nThe figure above shows part of the graph of $y=f(x)$ which is defined on the interval $x\\in [-3,3].$ Given that $f(x)$ is even, which of the following gives the complete graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of an even function must be symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\nSo, we can obtain the missing part of the graph by reflecting the given part over the $y$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the complete graph of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/22/22914a2693ec654474ca8572587386de4c35ae3feb6663bd1c915c29bbf9e5dd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d887d9f-b9c4-54b3-a53e-691989bda962" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Odd Functions Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb67f57-c9eb-5302-9978-48712d2b8472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/06d492280aea5ccc67bb39d3a61c2261e6c1c08a8e68220cf8a9041b8577da7e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519064} {:answer/id #uuid "645e8926-905a-5755-abc1-d624f15aa372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c91413057491ceb5a91ac2fa440df5507e222784fb142cc1adec219c5972095b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519139} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4bb28b-68d7-557a-9a50-0f96d5f4d5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :